    lead: 'A contract between an employer and employee setting out the terms of employment — role, compensation, benefits, work hours, intellectual property, confidentiality and how employment can end.',
    introParagraphs: ['An employment agreement (or employment contract) is signed when a new hire starts work. It sets out the key terms of the job — title, pay, hours, benefits, leave, confidentiality, IP assignment, non-compete/non-solicit and termination — so both sides know where they stand.', 'At-will employment is the default in most US states, but a written agreement is still useful for IP assignment, confidentiality, bonus/commission terms, and any restrictive covenants. Have an attorney review restrictive covenants as enforceability varies widely by state.'],
    bullets: [['Parties and role', 'Employer name and address; employee name and address; job title; department; start date; reports-to; work location (and remote-work terms).'], ['Compensation', 'Base salary or hourly rate, pay frequency, overtime eligibility, bonus/commission structure, equity if any, paydays.'], ['Benefits and leave', 'Health insurance, retirement plan, PTO/sick leave, holidays, parental leave, and other benefits (and when eligibility starts).'], ['Duties and expectations', 'Core responsibilities, work hours, at-will or fixed-term status, performance review schedule, and any probation period.'], ['Legal clauses', 'Intellectual property assignment, confidentiality, non-compete/non-solicit (as enforceable), return of property, dispute resolution (arbitration/mediation), and termination terms.']],
    howTo: [['Finalise the offer first', 'Agree on salary, title, start date and key terms verbally or in an offer letter before drafting the agreement.'], ['Use compliant language', 'Make sure restrictive covenants meet your state rules (non-competes are banned or unenforceable for many workers post-2024).'], ['Spell out compensation clearly', 'State base pay, bonus formula (discretionary vs formulaic), commission triggers and paydays explicitly.'], ['Include IP and confidentiality', 'Assign work product to the company and require confidentiality of company information; include a return-of-property clause.'], ['Sign before day one', 'Both parties sign before or on the start date; employee gets a fully signed copy. File with HR records.']],
    related: [['Job offer letter', 'templates/offer-letter-template.html', 'Shorter offer letter that often precedes the employment agreement.'], ['Employee NDA', 'templates/employee-nda.html', 'Confidentiality standalone if not inside the employment agreement.'], ['Employment verification letter', 'templates/employment-verification-template.html', 'Proof-of-employment letter for lenders and landlords.'], ['Non-compete agreement', 'templates/non-compete-agreement-template.html', 'Standalone restrictive covenant (check enforceability).'], ['Free HR letter templates', 'for/hr-teams.html', 'Full HR letter/contract template set.']],
    faq: [['Is an employment agreement required?', 'No in the US (at-will is the default) but strongly recommended for IP assignment, confidentiality and clear terms.'], ['Can I change it after signing?', 'Only with the employee written consent; material changes without consent can be unenforceable.'], ['Are non-competes enforceable?', 'They are banned for most workers under the 2024 FTC rule (currently under litigation) and heavily restricted in many states; consult counsel.'], ['Do I need one for every employee?', 'It is best practice for exempt/professional roles and any role with IP access; offer letters can suffice for hourly staff if you cover IP/confidentiality.'], ['At-will vs fixed-term?', 'At-will means either side can end it any time for a lawful reason; fixed-term sets an end date and usually requires cause or notice to terminate early.']],
